      "writtenOn": "2024-10-03T19:47:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The next CL I am about to uploading is to do some efforts to prevent `T` from being a non-primitive, non-POD type. I\u0027d like to also add a comment. As you pointed out, we don\u0027t call constructors. We do have some template meta programming pieces in resize-related functions; that (or similar) should be in the class scope, I guess.\n\nGiven that `T` is a POD or primitive, I think most compiler can at least map `std::copy` to `std::memcpy`. unless the compiler thinks there is something better (e.g. the compiler, unlike memcpy knows the alignment of `T` here).\n\nI guess, I found a similar argument from here:\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/4707012/is-it-better-to-use-stdmemcpy-or-stdcopy-in-terms-to-performance\n\nPerhaps, most of the users would build Android Emulator using Visual Studio, clang, and/or gcc, it might be okay to leave that decision to the compiler.",
